        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124654,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124655,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The head and neck are affected most frequently. Similar tumours on the glans penis may behave aggressively {26848799}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124656,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours, which often arise in elderly men, have no clinical features to distinguish them from conventional SCC.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124657,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This rare SCC type often occurs on sun-damaged skin of older men.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124658,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "<p>The risk factors appear to be similar to those for conventional SCC.</p>\r\n<h5>Genetic susceptibility</h5>\r\n<p>The genetic susceptibilities beyond those associated with conventional SCC are unknown.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124659,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is unknown. There is accumulation of glycogen in the cytoplasm of the neoplastic cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124660,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is similar to that of conventional SCC (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/64/35\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Squamous cell carcinomas</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124661,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 396,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumours contain malignant squamoid clear epithelial cells with cytoplasmic vacuolation and focal keratinization. There is no consensus on the required proportion of clear cells for the definition of clear cell SCC, but &gt;&nbsp;25% has been suggested {19463234}. The tumours may be invasive or in&nbsp;situ {17302606}. Intracytoplasmic vacuoles are typically unilocular, unlike in sebaceous neoplasms. The clear material is usually PAS-positive glycogen {18645304}. Some clear cell SCCs without glycogen have been identified {6163367}, with vesiculation possibly representing a degenerative phenomenon or artefact. When a single cytoplasmic vacuole compresses the nucleus, the lesions may be designated as signet-ring cell SCC {2467552; 19751229; 9550318; 24019773}. Immunohistochemistry is similar to that of conventional SCC; BerEP4 is negative.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>There is some morphological similarity between the histological appearances of clear cell SCC, signet-ring cell SCC, and trichilemmal carcinoma. Trichilemmal carcinoma is usually deeply seated, with no connection to the epidermis, and it may show peripheral palisading, basement membrane prominence, and pilar keratinization {7528473}. Sebaceous carcinoma shows multivacuolated cytoplasm with frequent positivity for perilipin, adipophilin, AR, and BerEP4 {20118912}. Clear cell basal cell carcinoma is BerEP4-positive. The presence of ducts, highlighted by staining for CEA and EMA, helps to identify porocarcinoma, hidradenocarcinoma, and dermal duct tumours. In clear cell atypical fibroxanthoma, cytokeratin staining is negative and keratinization is absent {26956561}. Clear cell and balloon cell melanomas stain for melanocyte markers. If there is no evidence of epidermal origin, metastatic carcinomas should be considered, especially from the kidney (PAX8-positive) {21552115}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:57:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
